WEBVTT

00:00.120 --> 00:01.350
Instructor: So, we just talked about working

00:01.350 --> 00:04.410
with collaborators and adding collaborators and all of that.

00:04.410 --> 00:05.910
Now, we're switching gears to talk about

00:05.910 --> 00:08.490
these things called README files.

00:08.490 --> 00:11.121
So, you may remember, and you've probably seen it before,

00:11.121 --> 00:15.540
when we make a repo on GitHub, if I just go new repository,

00:15.540 --> 00:18.060
I'm not gonna actually make one, one of the options

00:18.060 --> 00:21.180
of three options here to initialize a repository

00:21.180 --> 00:24.690
with a file, one of them is add a README file.

00:24.690 --> 00:25.530
So, what is that?

00:25.530 --> 00:27.570
README in all caps.

00:27.570 --> 00:29.640
Well, the README is a particular file

00:29.640 --> 00:33.210
that plays a special role on a GitHub repository.

00:33.210 --> 00:34.590
And it's not limited to GitHub,

00:34.590 --> 00:37.290
but in our context we're talking about GitHub.

00:37.290 --> 00:39.693
It's a file that is supposed to explain

00:39.693 --> 00:42.360
what your project does, whether you're working

00:42.360 --> 00:46.110
on some API or a web application, a video game,

00:46.110 --> 00:48.150
or even if it's just, you know,

00:48.150 --> 00:52.301
you're a government using GitHub to make laws and legislate.

00:52.301 --> 00:55.560
Whatever your repo is, the README file

00:55.560 --> 00:57.780
should include information about what the project does,

00:57.780 --> 00:59.520
what it's for, who's it for,

00:59.520 --> 01:02.040
how to get started with the project, how to run it

01:02.040 --> 01:04.440
and install dependencies if necessary,

01:04.440 --> 01:07.470
why it's noteworthy, who maintains the project,

01:07.470 --> 01:09.960
how to collaborate the project, how to get help.

01:09.960 --> 01:11.490
Not all of that has to be there,

01:11.490 --> 01:14.190
but if there's important information you want to convey

01:14.190 --> 01:17.850
to somebody coming to your project for the first time,

01:17.850 --> 01:19.800
then put it in the README file.

01:19.800 --> 01:22.020
So, README in all caps.

01:22.020 --> 01:24.420
You'll see them all over the place if you haven't,

01:24.420 --> 01:25.620
and they're very important

01:25.620 --> 01:27.210
because if you put a README file

01:27.210 --> 01:29.250
in the root of your project,

01:29.250 --> 01:31.200
GitHub is automatically going to display it.

01:31.200 --> 01:33.780
It will render the contents on the page for us.

01:33.780 --> 01:36.660
So, when I go to a repository like this,

01:36.660 --> 01:39.240
I just opened a couple trending repositories.

01:39.240 --> 01:41.610
This one here, Clean Architecture,

01:41.610 --> 01:45.600
it has a README file in the root of the directory

01:45.600 --> 01:48.630
and it's automatically displayed for me.

01:48.630 --> 01:53.010
As you can see, it tells me a title, a little brief

01:53.010 --> 01:55.560
about what it does, a list of technologies,

01:55.560 --> 01:57.810
how to get started, how to configure things,

01:57.810 --> 02:00.270
how to configure the database, how to migrate,

02:00.270 --> 02:03.030
all this stuff, keep going down, how to support,

02:03.030 --> 02:05.700
or how to get supports, the license

02:05.700 --> 02:07.230
that it's licensed under.

02:07.230 --> 02:09.840
Here's a different project, same deal.

02:09.840 --> 02:11.850
It's called, what is this spaCY?

02:11.850 --> 02:15.000
It's a tool for natural language processing in Python.

02:15.000 --> 02:17.520
Again, I just clicked a trending repo.

02:17.520 --> 02:20.880
It has a README file, and if I scroll down,

02:20.880 --> 02:23.640
that is what automatically GitHub detects

02:23.640 --> 02:27.570
and it renders for users coming to this main repo page.

02:27.570 --> 02:30.170
So, I see the title and all the stuff on the README.

02:31.020 --> 02:33.090
There's more information, where to ask questions,

02:33.090 --> 02:35.700
how to ask questions, features of this tool,

02:35.700 --> 02:40.230
how to install things, how to update, what else?

02:40.230 --> 02:43.410
How to compile from source, how to run the tests.

02:43.410 --> 02:46.590
Okay, so we're not limited just to software,

02:46.590 --> 02:50.040
although, of course, that's what a lot of GitHub repos are,

02:50.040 --> 02:52.680
but the README file, whatever is in it,

02:52.680 --> 02:54.360
is going to be automatically rendered

02:54.360 --> 02:57.515
if GitHub detects it in the root of your project.

02:57.515 --> 03:01.140
Also, I believe it will look in the dot git folder,

03:01.140 --> 03:02.820
but almost everyone I've seen

03:02.820 --> 03:05.430
puts it right in the root of the directory.

03:05.430 --> 03:07.860
All right, so READMEs play an important role.

03:07.860 --> 03:09.601
It's the first thing somebody looks

03:09.601 --> 03:11.340
at when they come to your project.

03:11.340 --> 03:13.890
I mean, the title says it all, README.

03:13.890 --> 03:14.940
That's the goal.

03:14.940 --> 03:15.810
That's the aim here.

03:15.810 --> 03:18.690
Someone visits our, let's say React

03:18.690 --> 03:20.490
'cause it's a very large repository,

03:20.490 --> 03:24.120
this is like the entry point for people to learn more.

03:24.120 --> 03:27.240
And often, it should include a bunch of links.

03:27.240 --> 03:30.270
You know, learn how to use React, online playground.

03:30.270 --> 03:33.120
So, this isn't even a very detailed README

03:33.120 --> 03:34.800
